# Kindle & Dofka - Dental Terminology.
# Chapter list: 4th edition (2022), from the publisher's table of contents on cengage.com.
# Summaries cover each chapter's standard topics; they are written for this site, not copied from the book.

C = [
    (1, "Introduction to Dental Terminology", [
        "Dental terms, like other medical terms, are built from word roots, combining forms, prefixes, and suffixes. Common dental word parts include dent/o and odont/o (tooth), gingiv/o (gums), or/o and stomat/o (mouth), gloss/o and lingu/o (tongue), cheil/o (lip), bucc/o (cheek), and ortho- (straight). Understanding word parts lets you decode unfamiliar terms.",
        "The dental team includes the dentist (DDS or DMD), dental hygienist, dental assistant, dental laboratory technician, and business staff. Dental specialties include orthodontics, periodontics, endodontics, prosthodontics, oral and maxillofacial surgery, pediatric dentistry, oral pathology, oral radiology, and dental public health.",
    ], [
        "dent/o, odont/o = tooth; gingiv/o = gums",
        "stomat/o = mouth; gloss/o = tongue; cheil/o = lip",
        "DDS and DMD are equivalent dentist degrees",
        "Dental team: dentist, hygienist, assistant, lab tech, office staff",
    ]),
    (2, "Anatomy and Oral Structures", [
        "The skull includes the cranium and facial bones; the maxilla (upper jaw) holds the upper teeth and the mandible (lower jaw) is the only movable skull bone, connecting at the temporomandibular joint (TMJ). Muscles of mastication include the masseter, temporalis, and medial and lateral pterygoids.",
        "The oral cavity includes the lips, cheeks, hard and soft palate, uvula, tongue, floor of the mouth, salivary glands (parotid, submandibular, sublingual), and gingiva. Each tooth has a crown and root; tissues are enamel, dentin, cementum, and pulp, supported by the periodontium (gingiva, periodontal ligament, cementum, alveolar bone). Adults have 32 permanent teeth; children have 20 primary teeth. Tooth surfaces include mesial, distal, facial (buccal or labial), lingual, and occlusal or incisal.",
    ], [
        "Mandible = only movable skull bone; TMJ",
        "Masseter is a main chewing muscle",
        "Enamel, dentin, cementum, pulp",
        "32 permanent, 20 primary teeth",
    ]),
    (3, "Tooth Origin and Formation", [
        "Teeth develop in stages: bud, cap, bell, apposition (laying down enamel and dentin matrix), and maturation (calcification). Ameloblasts form enamel, odontoblasts form dentin, and cementoblasts form cementum. Primary teeth begin forming before birth.",
        "Eruption is the movement of a tooth into the mouth; exfoliation is the shedding of primary teeth. Dentition periods are primary, mixed (both types present, about ages 6–12), and permanent. Developmental anomalies include anodontia (missing teeth), supernumerary teeth, macrodontia and microdontia, and enamel defects.",
    ], [
        "Bud, cap, bell, apposition, maturation",
        "Ameloblasts make enamel; odontoblasts make dentin",
        "Exfoliation = shedding primary teeth",
        "Mixed dentition ≈ ages 6–12",
    ]),
    (4, "Practice and Facility Setups", [
        "Dental practices can be solo, group, or specialty practices, as well as clinics and hospitals. The dental office includes the reception area, business office, treatment rooms (operatories), sterilization area, dental laboratory, and radiography area.",
        "Operatory equipment includes the dental chair, operator's and assistant's stools, the dental unit with handpieces, air-water syringe, high-volume evacuator (HVE) and saliva ejector, the operating light, and the curing light. Four-handed dentistry has the assistant and operator working together efficiently, with the clock concept describing their positions.",
    ], [
        "Operatory = treatment room",
        "HVE and saliva ejector remove fluids",
        "Four-handed dentistry: dentist and assistant work together",
        "Clock concept describes working positions",
    ]),
    (5, "Infection Control", [
        "Infection control prevents disease transmission in the dental office. Standard precautions treat all patients as potentially infectious. OSHA's Bloodborne Pathogens Standard requires PPE, hepatitis B vaccination for employees, sharps disposal, and an exposure control plan. The CDC issues dental infection control guidelines.",
        "Instruments are classified as critical (penetrate tissue, must be sterilized), semicritical (touch mucous membranes, sterilize or high-level disinfect), and noncritical (touch intact skin, disinfect). Sterilization methods include the steam autoclave, chemical vapor, and dry heat. Biological monitoring (spore tests) confirms sterilization. Surface barriers and disinfectants protect the operatory.",
    ], [
        "Standard precautions for all patients",
        "Critical items must be sterilized",
        "Autoclave = steam under pressure",
        "Spore tests confirm sterilization",
    ]),
    (6, "Emergency Care", [
        "Dental offices must be ready for medical emergencies. Preventing emergencies starts with a thorough medical history and vital signs. Every office keeps an emergency kit with oxygen, epinephrine, and other drugs, and staff maintain CPR certification.",
        "Common emergencies include syncope (fainting, the most common; position the patient supine with legs elevated), allergic reactions and anaphylaxis, asthma attacks, angina and heart attacks, hypoglycemia in diabetic patients, seizures, and airway obstruction. Dental emergencies include avulsed (knocked-out) teeth, fractured teeth, and postoperative bleeding.",
    ], [
        "Syncope = fainting, most common office emergency",
        "Take a medical history and vital signs",
        "Keep oxygen and epinephrine in the emergency kit",
        "Avulsed tooth = knocked out",
    ]),
    (7, "Examination and Prevention", [
        "The patient examination includes medical and dental history, vital signs, an extraoral and intraoral exam, charting of existing conditions, periodontal assessment, and radiographs. Charting uses tooth-numbering systems (Universal, Palmer, FDI) and symbols for restorations, caries, and missing teeth.",
        "Prevention includes brushing, flossing, fluoride (systemic and topical), sealants on pits and fissures, a healthy diet low in fermentable sugars, and regular cleanings (prophylaxis). Dental caries (cavities) are caused by bacteria such as Streptococcus mutans producing acid from sugars. Plaque that hardens becomes calculus (tartar).",
    ], [
        "Universal system numbers permanent teeth 1–32",
        "Sealants protect pits and fissures",
        "Caries caused by acid from bacteria",
        "Calculus = hardened plaque",
    ]),
    (8, "Pain Management and Pharmacology", [
        "Pain control in dentistry uses topical anesthetics (applied to the surface) and local anesthetics injected as infiltration or nerve blocks, such as the inferior alveolar nerve block for mandibular teeth. Vasoconstrictors such as epinephrine prolong anesthesia and reduce bleeding. Nitrous oxide-oxygen sedation relieves anxiety.",
        "Other options include oral or IV sedation and general anesthesia. Dental pharmacology covers prescriptions, analgesics, antibiotics (including premedication for some heart conditions), and drug interactions. Prescriptions include the superscription (Rx), inscription, subscription, and signature (directions).",
    ], [
        "Topical = surface; local = injected",
        "Inferior alveolar block numbs lower teeth",
        "Epinephrine prolongs anesthesia",
        "Nitrous oxide reduces anxiety",
    ]),
    (9, "Radiography", [
        "Dental radiographs (X-rays) show caries, bone loss, infections, and unerupted teeth. Intraoral images include bitewing (shows crowns of upper and lower teeth, good for caries between teeth), periapical (whole tooth and surrounding bone), and occlusal views. Extraoral images include panoramic and cephalometric radiographs.",
        "Radiation safety follows the ALARA principle (as low as reasonably achievable) using lead aprons with thyroid collars, fast film or digital sensors, collimation, and proper technique. Radiolucent areas (dark) let radiation pass, such as caries and pulp; radiopaque areas (light) block it, such as enamel and metal restorations.",
    ], [
        "Bitewing = crowns, caries between teeth",
        "Periapical = whole tooth and root",
        "ALARA principle",
        "Radiolucent = dark; radiopaque = light",
    ]),
    (10, "Tooth Restorations", [
        "Restorative dentistry repairs teeth damaged by caries or trauma. G.V. Black's classification describes cavity locations: Class I (pits and fissures), Class II (proximal surfaces of posterior teeth), Class III and IV (proximal surfaces of anterior teeth, IV including the incisal edge), Class V (cervical third), and Class VI (incisal edges or cusp tips).",
        "Restorative materials include amalgam (silver-colored, for posterior teeth), composite resin (tooth-colored, bonded), glass ionomer (releases fluoride), and cements, liners, and bases that protect the pulp. Procedures use the rubber dam for isolation, matrix bands for Class II, and dental handpieces and burs.",
    ], [
        "Class I = pits and fissures; Class II = posterior proximal",
        "Class V = cervical third",
        "Amalgam = silver; composite = tooth-colored",
        "Rubber dam isolates the tooth",
    ]),
    (11, "Cosmetic Dentistry", [
        "Cosmetic (esthetic) dentistry improves the appearance of teeth and smiles. Procedures include tooth whitening (in-office or at-home bleaching), composite bonding, porcelain veneers (thin shells bonded to the front of teeth), and crowns.",
        "Other procedures include enamel contouring (reshaping), gingival contouring, and implants. Shade selection, smile design, and patient expectations are important. Side effects of whitening can include tooth sensitivity.",
    ], [
        "Veneers = thin shells on front of teeth",
        "Bonding = composite applied to teeth",
        "Whitening can cause sensitivity",
        "Shade selection matches natural teeth",
    ]),
    (12, "Prosthodontics", [
        "Prosthodontics replaces missing teeth and restores damaged ones. Fixed prostheses include crowns (cover a tooth), bridges (replace missing teeth by attaching to abutment teeth; the replacement tooth is a pontic), inlays and onlays, and implants.",
        "Removable prostheses include complete dentures (all teeth in an arch) and partial dentures. Impressions, bite registrations, temporary crowns, and try-ins are part of the process. Dental implants are titanium posts placed in the bone that fuse through osseointegration.",
    ], [
        "Bridge: abutments support a pontic",
        "Complete denture replaces a full arch",
        "Implants fuse with bone (osseointegration)",
        "Onlay covers a cusp; inlay doesn't",
    ]),
    (13, "Endodontics", [
        "Endodontics treats the dental pulp and periapical tissues. Pulpitis (inflamed pulp) can be reversible or irreversible. Infection can spread to form a periapical abscess. Diagnosis uses pulp vitality tests (cold, heat, electric), percussion, palpation, and radiographs.",
        "Root canal treatment removes the infected pulp, cleans and shapes the canals with files, and fills them with gutta-percha and sealer. Other procedures include pulpotomy (removing the coronal pulp, often in children), pulpectomy, apicoectomy (removing the root tip), and pulp capping.",
    ], [
        "Pulpitis = inflamed pulp",
        "Root canal fills canals with gutta-percha",
        "Pulpotomy removes the coronal pulp",
        "Apicoectomy removes the root tip",
    ]),
    (14, "Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery", [
        "Oral and maxillofacial surgeons perform extractions (simple and surgical), removal of impacted third molars (wisdom teeth), biopsies, implant placement, treatment of jaw fractures, and corrective jaw surgery (orthognathic surgery).",
        "Surgical instruments include forceps, elevators, scalpels, rongeurs, bone files, curettes, and sutures. Post-extraction instructions help prevent dry socket (alveolar osteitis), which occurs when the blood clot is lost: avoid straws, smoking, and vigorous rinsing.",
    ], [
        "Impacted third molars = wisdom teeth",
        "Elevators loosen teeth; forceps remove them",
        "Dry socket = lost blood clot",
        "Avoid straws and smoking after extraction",
    ]),
    (15, "Orthodontics", [
        "Orthodontics corrects malocclusion (improper bite) and tooth alignment. Angle's classification describes occlusion: Class I (normal molar relationship, with crowding or spacing), Class II (distoclusion, lower jaw back, overjet), and Class III (mesioclusion, lower jaw forward, underbite).",
        "Appliances include fixed braces (brackets, bands, archwires, ligatures), clear aligners, retainers, headgear, and palatal expanders. Terms include overbite (vertical overlap), overjet (horizontal overlap), crossbite, and open bite. Cephalometric radiographs and models help plan treatment.",
    ], [
        "Malocclusion = improper bite",
        "Class II = overjet; Class III = underbite",
        "Overbite vertical; overjet horizontal",
        "Retainers hold teeth after treatment",
    ]),
    (16, "Periodontics", [
        "Periodontics treats the supporting structures of the teeth. Gingivitis is reversible inflammation of the gums caused by plaque; periodontitis involves loss of attachment and bone and can lead to tooth loss. Risk factors include smoking, diabetes, poor hygiene, and genetics.",
        "Assessment uses a periodontal probe to measure pocket depth (1–3 mm is normal), bleeding, mobility, and furcation involvement. Treatment includes scaling and root planing, antimicrobial therapy, gingivectomy, flap surgery, and grafts. Periodontal maintenance visits keep disease under control.",
    ], [
        "Gingivitis is reversible; periodontitis destroys bone",
        "Normal pocket depth 1–3 mm",
        "Scaling and root planing remove calculus",
        "Smoking and diabetes raise risk",
    ]),
    (17, "Pediatric Dentistry", [
        "Pediatric dentists treat infants, children, adolescents, and patients with special needs. Behavior management techniques include tell-show-do, positive reinforcement, and voice control. Early childhood caries (baby bottle tooth decay) results from prolonged exposure to sugary liquids.",
        "Preventive care includes fluoride varnish, sealants, and space maintainers when primary teeth are lost early. Pulp therapy (pulpotomy), stainless steel crowns, and treatment of traumatic injuries are common. Dentists must recognize and report suspected child abuse.",
    ], [
        "Tell-show-do behavior management",
        "Early childhood caries from bottles at bedtime",
        "Space maintainers hold space for permanent teeth",
        "Stainless steel crowns on primary molars",
    ]),
    (18, "Dental Laboratory Materials", [
        "Dental materials have properties such as strength, hardness, elasticity, thermal conductivity, and biocompatibility. Impression materials include alginate (irreversible hydrocolloid) for study models, and elastomers such as polyvinyl siloxane for precise impressions.",
        "Gypsum products (plaster, dental stone, die stone) make models and casts. Waxes are used for patterns and bite registrations. Other materials include acrylic resins for dentures and custom trays, metals and alloys, ceramics, and cements. Correct mixing ratios and setting times matter.",
    ], [
        "Alginate = irreversible hydrocolloid",
        "Polyvinyl siloxane for precise impressions",
        "Plaster, stone, die stone = gypsum products",
        "Acrylic for dentures and custom trays",
    ]),
    (19, "Dental Laboratory Procedures", [
        "Dental laboratory procedures include pouring and trimming study models, fabricating custom impression trays, making bleaching trays, mouthguards, and night guards, and making temporary crowns.",
        "Laboratory technicians also fabricate crowns, bridges, dentures, and orthodontic appliances from prescriptions (lab work orders) sent by the dentist. Infection control applies to impressions and appliances sent to and from the lab, which must be disinfected.",
    ], [
        "Study models poured in plaster or stone",
        "Custom trays made from acrylic",
        "Lab work order = prescription to the lab",
        "Disinfect impressions before sending",
    ]),
    (20, "Business Management Procedures", [
        "The business office manages appointment scheduling, patient records, recall systems, accounts receivable and payable, insurance claims, inventory, and communication. Dental procedures are coded with CDT codes (Current Dental Terminology) for insurance billing.",
        "Dental records are legal documents and must be accurate, complete, and confidential under HIPAA. Good telephone and written communication, management of collections, and ethics and professionalism support the practice. Computerized practice management software handles many tasks.",
    ], [
        "CDT codes = dental procedure codes",
        "Recall system brings patients back",
        "Dental records are legal documents",
        "HIPAA protects patient information",
    ]),
]

